  },
  {
    "id": "wen-df360ix",
    "name": "WEN DF360iX 3600-Watt Dual Fuel RV-Ready Inverter Generator with CO Watchdog",
    "category": "Compact Dual Fuel Inverter, 2,900W Running / 3,600W Surge",
    "badge": "Best Portable Dual Fuel",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "About 49 lb, the lightest generator here by a wide margin",
      "Dual fuel: 2,900 rated watts on gasoline, 2,600 on propane",
      "CO Watchdog shutoff and a fuel shutoff to run the carburetor dry",
      "TT-30R RV outlet, two 120V outlets, 12V and USB",
      "3-year limited warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Only 2,900 running watts despite the 3600 model name",
      "1.5 gallon tank means frequent refueling on gasoline"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The carry-it-anywhere option for camping and RVs, with propane and a CO shutoff, but not a true 3,500 watt backup generator.",
    "description": "The WEN DF360iX is on this list for buyers who want the 3,600 watt label in something one person can lift. At about 49 pounds it weighs half as much as the open-frame picks, and it brings features that usually cost more: dual fuel with a tool-free propane connection, a CO Watchdog shutoff, a fuel shutoff that lets you run the carburetor dry before storage, and a TT-30R for an RV alongside two 120 volt outlets, a 12 volt outlet and USB ports. The honest caveat is in the ratings. WEN lists 3,600 surge watts but 2,900 rated watts on gasoline and 2,600 on propane. That is a capable camping and RV generator, enough for a 30-amp trailer without the air conditioner, and a good light backup for a refrigerator, lights and electronics. It is not the machine for a fridge, furnace and 1/2 horsepower sump pump all at once. The 1.5 gallon tank also means more refueling on gasoline, which is one reason to run it on a 20 pound propane cylinder. Owners commonly describe it as reasonably quiet but not whisper quiet. Buy it if portability ranks first."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best 3500 Watt Generator",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-3500-watt-generator/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What will a 3500 watt generator run?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A 3500 watt generator runs a refrigerator, a gas furnace blower, LED lighting, a router, a television, chargers and a 1/2 horsepower sump pump, provided you stagger the starts. It will not run central air conditioning, an electric water heater, an electric range, an electric dryer or a 240 volt well pump. It also runs most 30-amp RVs without the rooftop air conditioner."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a 3500 watt generator run a refrigerator and a sump pump?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, in most cases. A refrigerator runs at around 700 watts and a 1/2 horsepower sump pump at around 1,050, which is well within 3,500 running watts. The tight moment is when the sump pump starts while the fridge compressor is running. Generators with 4,375 or more starting watts usually cover it; staggering starts helps."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a 3500 watt generator run a 30 amp RV?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It will run nearly everything on a 30 amp RV except, in many cases, the rooftop air conditioner. A TT-30 outlet tops out at 3,600 watts, so a 3500W generator can supply most of what the shore cord carries. An open-frame unit often starts a 13,500 BTU AC on its own, while a compact inverter usually needs a soft starter."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a 3500 watt generator enough for a house?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is enough for the essentials in a house with gas heat: food storage, furnace blower, lights, internet and the sump pump. It is not enough to run a house normally. Central air, electric heat, an electric water heater and cooking appliances all need a larger generator, usually 7,500 watts or more, and often 240 volts."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between a 3500 and a 3600 watt generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Very little in practice. Model names reflect running watts, so the Westinghouse WGen3600cv is rated 3,600 running and 4,650 peak watts while the Champion 201489 is rated 3,500 running and 4,375 starting. The extra peak watts on the Westinghouse are more useful than the extra 100 running watts, because surge is what limits this class."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long will a 3500 watt generator run on a tank of gas?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most open-frame models are rated for 10 to 14 hours on a 4 to 4.7 gallon tank, and open-frame inverters for up to 22 hours, but those figures are quoted at 25 or 50 percent load. For an essentials load, plan on roughly one tank overnight and about 5 to 6 gallons per day of continuous use."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a generator with a CO shutoff?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "We recommend one. A CO shutoff sensor stops the engine if carbon monoxide builds up around the generator, which protects against the most common fatal mistake: running it in a garage or too close to the house. It does not make indoor use safe, and you still need CO alarms inside the home on every sleeping level."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Emergency Preparedness",
    tags: [
      "3500 watt generator",
      "3500w generator",
      "generator 3500w",
      "3600 watt generator",
      "best 3500 watt generator",
      "generator with co shutoff"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">3500 Watt</span> Generators
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          The size that keeps a refrigerator, a gas furnace blower, lights and a sump pump going, as long as you understand which of them is allowed to start at the same time.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#champion-201489-remote-start"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Compare the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="9.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-it-runs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What 3,500 Watts Runs</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The 5 Best Picks</a>
          <a href="#essentials-math" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The Essentials Math</a>
          <a href="#sump-pump" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The Sump Pump Problem</a>
          <a href="#rv-30-amp" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. 30A RV Without Rooftop AC</a>
