          <a href="#co-shutoff" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. CO Shutoff Models</a>
          <a href="#fuel-runtime" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Fuel, Runtime and Propane</a>
          <a href="#connecting" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Connecting to the House</a>
          <a href="#who-should-skip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Who Should Skip This Size</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. Common Mistakes</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A 3500 watt generator is the smallest size that can carry the four loads most outages are really about: the refrigerator, the furnace blower, a few lights and the sump pump. It is also the size where the spec sheet and reality drift apart the most, because 3,500 running watts is plenty and 4,375 starting watts is barely enough. Here is what fits, what does not, and the five models worth buying.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-it-runs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a 3500 Watt Generator Actually Runs
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Running and Starting Watts at This Size</h3>

          <p>
            A 3500 watt generator is rated for roughly 3,500 running watts and somewhere between 4,000 and 4,650 starting watts, depending on the brand. The running figure is what it can carry all day. The starting figure is a short burst, measured in seconds, that it can deliver when a motor or compressor kicks on. Almost every buying mistake at this size comes from reading the bigger number and planning around it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What 3,500 Running Watts Covers</h3>

          <p>
            In practical terms, 3,500 running watts covers a full-size refrigerator, a chest freezer, the blower and controls on a gas or propane furnace, LED lighting across most of a house, a router, phone chargers, a television and a sump pump. It does not cover central air conditioning, an electric water heater, an electric range, an electric dryer or a well pump of any meaningful size. Those loads either need 240 volts, more than 3,500 watts, or both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">An Essentials Machine, Not a Whole-House Machine</h3>

          <p>
            That makes the 3500W generator a genuine essentials machine, not a whole-house machine. It is the right size for a household with gas heat that wants the food cold, the basement dry and the lights on through a winter storm. It is also the right size for a 30-amp travel trailer that does not need its rooftop air conditioner, or that has a soft starter fitted to it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Size Sells: Weight and Price</h3>

          <p>
            The other reason this size sells is weight and price. A 3,500 watt open-frame generator typically weighs about 100 pounds and costs a fraction of a 7,500 watt unit. It burns less fuel per hour, fits a smaller shed, and can be rolled out by one person. If your essentials list is short, there is no point carrying around the extra engine. Our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a> lists typical figures for more than forty appliances if you want to check your own list before reading further.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Class at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3,500W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical running watts</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">4,375W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical starting watts</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~100 lb</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Open-frame weight</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3,600W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">TT-30 RV outlet ceiling</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The 5 Best 3500 Watt Generators
