        <div id="essentials-math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Fridge, Furnace, Lights and Sump Math
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Essentials List, Load by Load</h3>

          <p>
            Here is the essentials list worked through with the typical figures from our wattage chart. A refrigerator runs at about 700 watts and surges to about 2,200 when its compressor starts. A gas furnace blower and its controls draw 600 to 900 watts, with a modest surge. LED lighting, a router, a television and chargers add roughly 400 watts. A 1/2 horsepower sump pump runs at about 1,050 watts and can surge to 2,600.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Adding Up the Running Watts</h3>

          <p>
            Add the running figures and you get roughly 2,700 to 3,050 watts with everything on at once. That fits under 3,500 running watts with a little room to spare. The problem is the starting side. If the refrigerator, furnace and lights are all running at around 2,000 watts and the sump pump kicks on, the generator briefly has to supply about 2,000 plus 2,600, or 4,600 watts. That is above the 4,375 starting watts most 3500W generators are rated for.
          </p>

