          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Worst Case Is Not Constant</h3>

          <p>
            In real life the worst case is not constant. A refrigerator compressor runs perhaps a third of the time, a furnace blower cycles with the thermostat, and a sump pump only starts when the pit fills. Most of the time the surges do not line up. But in a storm, when the sump is cycling every few minutes, they eventually will, and a generator that cannot carry the overlap trips its breaker or stalls the pump motor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three Ways to Live With the Overlap</h3>

          <p>
            There are three honest ways to live with this. Buy a model with a higher starting rating, such as the 4,650 peak watt Westinghouse below, which covers most of the overlap. Trim the list, for example by unplugging the refrigerator for the hours when the sump is busiest, since a closed fridge holds temperature for hours. Or step up to a larger generator. Our walkthrough on <a href="/can-a-generator-power-a-furnace-refrigerator-and-sump-pump/">powering a furnace, refrigerator and sump pump together</a> covers the overlap in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sump-pump" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why the Sump Pump Decides Whether 3,500 Watts Is Enough
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The One Load You Cannot Defer</h3>

          <p>
            In most basements the sump pump is the largest single surge on the backup list, and it is the one load you cannot defer. A refrigerator can sit closed for four hours. A furnace can be turned down. A sump pump that fails to start during a heavy rain lets water into the basement within the hour. Size the generator around the pump first, then fit the other loads around it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Know Your Pump's Horsepower</h3>

          <p>
            Start by finding out what you actually have. A 1/3 horsepower pump typically runs around 800 watts and surges well under 2,000, which a 3500W generator handles comfortably alongside the rest of the list. A 1/2 horsepower pump is the common case and is the borderline one described above. A 3/4 horsepower or 1 horsepower pump, often fitted in houses with high water tables, can surge past 3,000 watts on its own. If that is your pump, a 3500 watt generator is the wrong size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Pump Age Matters</h3>

          <p>
            Pump age matters too. An older pump with worn bearings or a clogged impeller draws more on startup than its label suggests, and owners commonly report pumps that start fine on grid power but stall on a small generator. Running the pump on the generator for a test cycle, by pouring a bucket of water into the pit, is the simplest way to find out before you need it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Half-Horsepower Pump Plus a Well</h3>

          <p>
            If your pump is 1/2 horsepower and the house also has a well, stop here. A shallow-well jet pump or a submersible well pump adds another large surge, and most well pumps want 240 volts, which no 3500W generator in this guide provides. Our guides to <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-a-sump-pump/">what size generator runs a sump pump</a> and the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators for power outages</a> cover the bigger setups.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Let the sump pump start first, then add the rest.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">After a restart, plug in the sump pump, let it cycle once, then add the furnace and finally the refrigerator. Staggering the starts is free, and it is often the difference between a 3500W generator coping and tripping.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="rv-30-amp" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Running a 30-Amp RV Without the Rooftop AC
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The TT-30R Ceiling</h3>

          <p>
            A 30-amp RV plugs into a TT-30R receptacle, which is a 120 volt, 30 amp outlet. Thirty amps at 120 volts is 3,600 watts, so a 3500W generator can deliver nearly everything a 30-amp shore cord is designed to carry. All five generators below have a 30 amp RV outlet built in, which means the RV cord plugs straight in with no adapter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Trailer Draws Without the AC</h3>

          <p>
            Without the air conditioner running, a typical 30-amp trailer draws far less than that. The converter that charges the house batteries, the refrigerator on electric mode, the water heater element, the furnace blower, the water pump, lights, a television and a microwave can all be managed on 3,500 watts if you avoid running the microwave and the water heater element at the same moment. Most RV owners find a 3500W generator generous for boondocking in cool weather.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Rooftop Air Conditioner Exception</h3>

          <p>
            The rooftop air conditioner is the exception. A 13,500 BTU RV unit typically runs at around 1,500 to 1,800 watts but can surge well past 3,000 watts when its compressor starts, especially in hot weather or at altitude. An open-frame 3500W generator with 4,375 or more starting watts usually starts it, provided little else is running. A 3000-running-watt inverter often does not, unless the AC has a soft starter fitted.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How a Soft Starter Helps</h3>

          <p>
            A soft starter is a small module wired into the air conditioner that ramps the compressor up gradually instead of slamming it on. Soft start makers advertise large cuts to the starting surge, and owners commonly report that a soft-started 13,500 BTU unit runs on generators that could not start it before. If you camp in heat and want to stay at this size, fit one. If you would rather not modify the RV, the <a href="/best-4000-watt-generator/">4000 watt class</a> is the safer buy, and our <a href="/best-rv-generator/">RV generator guide</a> covers 50-amp rigs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="open-frame-vs-inverter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Open-Frame vs Inverter at 3,500 Watts
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two Machines, One Wattage Label</h3>

          <p>
            At this size you choose between two very different machines that share a wattage label. A conventional open-frame generator runs its engine at a fixed 3,600 rpm to produce 60 hertz power directly. An inverter generator produces raw power at whatever speed the load needs, then converts it electronically to clean 120 volt AC. Our comparison of <a href="/inverter-generator-vs-regular-generator/">inverter generators vs regular generators</a> goes through the engineering; here is what it means for buying in the 3500W class.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Open-Frame Units Win</h3>

          <p>
            Open-frame units give you more starting watts per dollar. The Champion 201489 and the Westinghouse WGen3600 pair below list 4,375 and 4,650 starting watts at prices well under most inverters. That surge capacity is exactly what a sump pump or RV air conditioner needs. The trade-offs are noise, fuel use at light load and power quality. They run at full speed even when only a refrigerator is plugged in, which is louder and burns more fuel than necessary.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Inverters Win</h3>

          <p>
            Inverter units are quieter, more fuel efficient at light load and produce cleaner power, which matters for furnace control boards, variable-speed appliances and electronics. The catch at this size is that many 3500-class inverters carry lower running ratings. The WEN DF360iX, for example, is rated for 2,900 running and 3,600 surge watts, and plenty of suitcase inverters sold as 3500 or 3800 watt models sit around 3,000 running watts. They are excellent for camping and light backup, but they do not have the surge headroom of a 3500W open-frame unit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Open-Frame Inverter Compromise</h3>

          <p>
            The open-frame inverter, such as the Champion 200977, is the compromise. It pairs a tubular frame with inverter electronics and economy mode, and Champion rates it at 3,500 running and 4,250 starting watts at 64 dBA, on gasoline or propane. It costs more than an open-frame conventional unit and less than a fully enclosed inverter of the same output.
          </p>

