        <div id="co-shutoff" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. CO Shutoff Models: Why We Treat It as Standard
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why a CO Shutoff Matters</h3>

          <p>
            A portable generator produces carbon monoxide at a rate that can kill in minutes in an enclosed space, and generator misuse during outages is a recurring cause of CO deaths in the United States. Industry safety standards now push manufacturers toward automatic CO shutoff, and most major brands sell a version of their popular models with a sensor that stops the engine if CO builds up around the unit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which Picks Have One</h3>

          <p>
            Four of our five picks have one: the Champion 201489 carries Champion's CO Shield, the Westinghouse WGen3600DFc and WGen3600cv carry Westinghouse's CO sensor, and the WEN DF360iX carries WEN's CO Watchdog. The Champion 200977 inverter does not list one, which is the main thing it gives up. The sensor monitors CO concentration around the generator itself and shuts it down if levels climb past a threshold, which mainly protects against the generator being run in a garage, a shed, a carport with walls or too close to an open door.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Not Permission to Run It Indoors</h3>

          <p>
            A CO shutoff is not permission to run the generator indoors or in an attached garage. The manuals for these models say the same thing: outdoors only, away from windows, doors and vents. The sensor is a backstop for the mistake that happens at 2 a.m. in a storm, not a way to bring the generator closer. You still need battery CO alarms inside the house on every sleeping level.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Placement Does More Than Any Sensor</h3>

          <p>
            Placement does more for safety than any sensor. Run the generator at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from openings, on a firm surface and under a proper cover if it is raining. Our guide on <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a> covers distances, covers and wind direction.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel-runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Fuel, Runtime and Running on Propane
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Load Behind Runtime Figures</h3>

          <p>
            Runtime figures on spec sheets are quoted at 25 or 50 percent load, and you need to check which before comparing. The Westinghouse WGen3600DFc is rated for up to 14 hours on 4 gallons of gasoline at 25 percent load. The Champion 200977 open-frame inverter is rated for up to 22 hours at 25 percent load. The WEN DF360iX, with a 1.5 gallon tank, will need refilling far sooner. Always compare at the same load.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Real Essentials Load and Daily Gasoline</h3>

          <p>
            For an essentials setup, expect the real average load to sit between 25 and 50 percent, because the refrigerator and furnace cycle on and off. In practice that means an overnight run on one tank for most of these generators, and roughly 5 to 6 gallons of gasoline per day for a 3500W open-frame unit running around the clock. Store fuel accordingly, treat it with stabilizer, and rotate it every few months.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Propane Solves the Storage Problem</h3>

          <p>
            Propane solves the storage problem. It does not go stale, and a standard 20 pound cylinder is easy to buy and store outdoors. The Westinghouse WGen3600DFc, Champion 200977 and WEN DF360iX are dual fuel. The cost is output: Westinghouse rates the WGen3600DFc at 3,240 running and 4,180 peak watts on propane, about 10 percent below its gasoline ratings, the Champion drops from 3,500 to 3,150 running watts, and the WEN from 2,900 to 2,600. On propane, the sump pump overlap described above gets harder, not easier.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan Enough Cylinders</h3>

          <p>
            A 20 pound cylinder holds about 4.6 gallons of propane, and a 3500W generator at moderate load burns through one in well under a day. If you plan to rely on propane, keep several cylinders or connect a larger tank. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-propane-does-a-generator-use/">how much propane a generator uses</a> has the consumption figures, and the <a href="/best-propane-generator/">best propane generators</a> guide covers models built around LP.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="connecting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Connecting a 3500W Generator to the House
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Extension Cords: Simple and Cheap</h3>

          <p>
            There are two ways to get generator power to the loads that need it. The simple way is extension cords: one heavy cord to the refrigerator, another to a power strip for lights and electronics, and one to the sump pump. It works, needs no electrician and costs little. Use a 12 gauge cord for runs up to about 50 feet carrying the refrigerator or sump pump, and a 10 gauge cord for anything longer. Our <a href="/generator-extension-cord-size/">generator extension cord size guide</a> has the full table.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Furnace Problem</h3>

          <p>
            The problem with cords is the furnace. A gas furnace is usually hardwired, so it cannot be plugged into an extension cord without rewiring. That is where a transfer switch or an interlock kit comes in, letting the generator feed selected house circuits through the panel safely. Our guide to <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch types and costs</a> compares the options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Generator's Outlet</h3>

          <p>
            Here the outlet matters. Most 3500W generators provide 120 volts only. The Champion 201489 has an L5-30R, a 120 volt 30 amp twist-lock outlet that pairs with 120 volt transfer switches and some inlet boxes, alongside its TT-30R. None of the five picks has an L14-30R, which is the 120/240 volt outlet that most whole-panel interlock kits expect. If you want to back-feed a panel through an interlock, check the wiring with an electrician before buying, or look at the larger units in our <a href="/best-portable-generator/">portable generator guide</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Back-Feed Through a Wall Outlet</h3>

          <p>
            Never plug a generator into a wall outlet with a double-ended cord. Back-feeding through an outlet can energize the utility line and endanger line workers, and it can overload the outlet wiring. A small manual switch for four to six circuits is the usual answer at this size; see our <a href="/best-manual-transfer-switch/">best manual transfer switches</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Skip the 3500 Watt Class
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If You Want to Run Central Air</h3>

          <p>
            If your house has central air conditioning you want to run, a 3500W generator will not do it. Even a small 2 ton central unit typically needs 240 volts and a starting surge beyond this class. Our guide to <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-central-air-conditioning/">what size generator runs central air</a> has the numbers, and the answer usually starts at 7,500 watts with a soft starter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If You Heat With Electricity</h3>

          <p>
            If you heat with electricity, the same applies. An electric furnace or a heat pump on emergency strip heat draws 10,000 watts or more. A 3500W generator can run the air handler fan but not the heat. In that case consider a much larger generator or plan to heat one room with a different source.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If You Only Need a Few Small Loads</h3>

          <p>
            If you only need to keep a refrigerator, a CPAP and phones going for a day, you may not need a gas generator at all. A large portable power station can handle those loads silently and indoors, though it will not start a sump pump for long. Our comparison of <a href="/portable-power-station-vs-generator/">power stations vs generators</a> and our look at <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-gas-furnace/">whether a power station can run a gas furnace</a> cover that route.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If You Camp With a Rooftop AC</h3>

          <p>
            And if you mainly camp in hot weather with a rooftop air conditioner and do not want a soft starter, buy the next size up. The 4000 to 4500 watt class adds the surge that a 13,500 BTU unit needs at only modest extra weight. For tent camping and tailgating, the opposite is true: a 2,000 watt inverter is lighter and quieter, and our <a href="/best-generator-for-camping/">camping generator guide</a> covers that end.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What will a 3500 watt generator run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 3500 watt generator runs a refrigerator, a gas furnace blower, LED lighting, a router, a television, chargers and a 1/2 horsepower sump pump, provided you stagger the starts. It will not run central air conditioning, an electric water heater, an electric range, an electric dryer or a 240 volt well pump. It also runs most 30-amp RVs without the rooftop air conditioner.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 3500 watt generator run a refrigerator and a sump pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, in most cases. A refrigerator runs at around 700 watts and a 1/2 horsepower sump pump at around 1,050, which is well within 3,500 running watts. The tight moment is when the sump pump starts while the fridge compressor is running. Generators with 4,375 or more starting watts usually cover it; staggering starts helps.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 3500 watt generator run a 30 amp RV?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It will run nearly everything on a 30 amp RV except, in many cases, the rooftop air conditioner. A TT-30 outlet tops out at 3,600 watts, so a 3500W generator can supply most of what the shore cord carries. An open-frame unit often starts a 13,500 BTU AC on its own, while a compact inverter usually needs a soft starter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 3500 watt generator enough for a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is enough for the essentials in a house with gas heat: food storage, furnace blower, lights, internet and the sump pump. It is not enough to run a house normally. Central air, electric heat, an electric water heater and cooking appliances all need a larger generator, usually 7,500 watts or more, and often 240 volts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a 3500 and a 3600 watt generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Very little in practice. Model names reflect running watts, so the Westinghouse WGen3600cv is rated 3,600 running and 4,650 peak watts while the Champion 201489 is rated 3,500 running and 4,375 starting. The extra peak watts on the Westinghouse are more useful than the extra 100 running watts, because surge is what limits this class.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a 3500 watt generator run on a tank of gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most open-frame models are rated for 10 to 14 hours on a 4 to 4.7 gallon tank, and open-frame inverters for up to 22 hours, but those figures are quoted at 25 or 50 percent load. For an essentials load, plan on roughly one tank overnight and about 5 to 6 gallons per day of continuous use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a generator with a CO shutoff?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We recommend one. A CO shutoff sensor stops the engine if carbon monoxide builds up around the generator, which protects against the most common fatal mistake: running it in a garage or too close to the house. It does not make indoor use safe, and you still need CO alarms inside the home on every sleeping level.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing to the starting watts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The large number on the box is a surge rating that lasts seconds. Plan your continuous load against running watts and keep it under about 80 percent of that figure, then check that your biggest motor can start on top of everything else.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every 3500 is a 3500</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many compact inverters sold in this class are rated 2,900 to 3,000 running watts. That is fine for camping but not the same machine as a 3,500 running watt open-frame unit. Read the running figure, not the model name.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the propane derate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dual fuel generator loses roughly 10 percent of its output on propane. A plan that only just fits on gasoline will trip the breaker on LP, usually at the moment the sump pump starts during the worst of the storm.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it in the garage with the door open</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An open garage door does not ventilate a running generator. CO builds up and flows into the house. A CO shutoff sensor is a backstop, not a license; run it 20 feet out with the exhaust facing away.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never test-running the sump pump</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many pumps that start fine on grid power stall on a small generator. Pour a bucket into the pit and run one full cycle on the generator before the storm season, not during it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

