        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A 3500 watt generator is the right buy when your list is the refrigerator, the furnace blower, the lights and the sump pump, or a 30-amp RV in cool weather. For most buyers that means the Champion 201489: CO Shield, a key fob, a TT-30R and an L5-30R, and enough starting watts for the common case. If your sump pump is the worry or you want propane, the Westinghouse WGen3600DFc buys you the extra peak watts and a second fuel.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Be honest about the limits. If the plan only works on paper when nothing starts at the same time, or if it includes a rooftop air conditioner in summer, central air or electric heat, this class is too small. Step up to 4,000 watts or more rather than spend every outage managing the breaker.
          </p>
